What is a Tuple?

A tuple is a collection of items in Python, just like a list.

👉 The big difference:
Tuples cannot be changed after creation (they are immutable).
Think of a tuple as a read-only list.

How to Create a Tuple
1️⃣ Using parentheses
my_tuple = (1, 2, 3)

2️⃣ Without parentheses (Python allows this)
my_tuple = 1, 2, 3

3️⃣ Single-element tuple (important!)
single = (5,)   # comma is mandatory


❌ (5) → this is just an integer, not a tuple

Tuple vs List (Quick Comparison)
| Feature                   | List  | Tuple |
| ------------------------- | ----- | ----- |
| Syntax                    | `[ ]` | `( )` |
| Mutable                   | ✅ Yes | ❌ No  |
| Can append/remove         | ✅     | ❌     |
| Faster                    | ❌     | ✅     |
| Safer (data won’t change) | ❌     | ✅     |

Slicing a Tuple

Same as lists:

numbers = (10, 20, 30, 40, 50)

print(numbers[1:4])   # (20, 30, 40)

------

Tuple Packing & Unpacking (🔥 Very Useful)
Packing
data = ("Sudhanshu", 25, "India")

Unpacking
name, age, country = data

print(name)     # Sudhanshu
print(age)      # 25
print(country)  # India


👉 This is used a LOT in interviews and real code.